WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:03.909 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  trash heaps near south asian mega cities  like new delhi dhaka and lahore are  threatening the climate  the piles of garbage are contributing to  some of the world's strongest most  persistent greenhouse gas emissions  observed by satellite the landfills are  leaking methane planet wrecking gas that  is generated when organic material like  food scraps decompose in the absence of  oxygen  near india's capital new delhi the  gajapur landfill was spewing methane at  a rate of 2.17 metric tons an hour when  a satellite from a missions monitoring  firm  ghgsat passed overhead  if that emissions rate was sustained  over a year the leak would have the same  short-term climate impact as the annual  emissions from about 350 000 u.s cars  waste experts say the key to avoiding  methane emissions from dumps is to  separate organic material from other  waste before it enters a landfill one of  the simplest solutions is to compost  food scraps  that approach is working in the southern  indian state of kerala where authorities  are helping local municipalities and  villages implement decentralized solid  waste management systems that promote  composting at household levels  south korea also successfully banned  food from landfills and now recycles  more than 90 percent of its food  new technology is also being used to  separate mixed waste streams  ontario-based energia has developed a  system that functions a bit like a giant  garlic crusher with a metal piston that  squeezes out more than 90 wet organic  material the paste is then placed into  an anaerobic digester where microbes  break it down and methane is captured  as the world grapples with how to  balance shared climate goals against the  backdrop of historical emissions that  generated tremendous wealth and  prosperity for rich western countries  many developing nations are still  playing catch-up and prioritizing things  like food security and basic services  but experts say limiting the climate  impact of waste is as much about  behavior than it is access to technology  and significant progress can be made  even with limited resources  [Music]

